[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50154029", :title "Launch HN: Laminar (YC S24) – RL-trained model that finds errors in agent traces", :link "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50154029", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T16:00:18.000-00:00", :content "Hi HN, we’re Robert and Din from Laminar (https://laminar.sh). We’ve been building open-source observability for AI agents since 2024. Recently, we released flow-1, a model trained with RL to find errors in agent traces. On our benchmark (https://laminar.sh/blog/flow-1), it matches GPT-6-sol in detection quality while being 23x cheaper. This makes it affordable to analyze every production agent trace, without sampling. Here’s a one-minute walkthrough: https://youtu.be/KJa3h6ZJNLQ We also put together a post with example traces and analyses from flow-1 and other models: https://laminar.sh/blog/flow-1-in-action Agent observability is crowded, so instead of a bullet list of how Laminar differs from X, we’ll explain how we’re solving agent observability from first principles. Agents now run for many steps and fail implicitly. An agent can click the wrong button or cite the wrong document while every call completes successfully. These errors are the most useful ones to catch and understand. To find them, you need to understand what the agent was trying to do and inspect its LLM calls and tool results. Simply looking at the final agent output is not enough. Collecting traces alone doesn’t solve this. A lot of agent observability platforms are still data collectors at their core. You can end up paying to store traces that nobody has time to read or extract value from. Using another LLM to inspect traces is THE solution, but capable models are expensive at scale and cheaper ones often miss errors or flag unrelated things. So we built our model to solve exactly this problem. We finetuned a small OSS model and then post trained it with RL on thousands of synthetic investigations of realistic agent workflows in software, legal, customer support, etc. We got a model that is both good at agent trace analysis and very cheap to run. flow-1 runs inside a harness we call Signals. It acts as a coding agent, treating an agent trace like a repo, with each span (an LLM call, tool call, etc.) as a file. It starts from a compressed string representation of a full agent trace and a user prompt, then greps across spans and reads the relevant ones in full. The beauty of Signals is that it can respond to a general prompt. You can ask it to identify user frustration, check whether your browser agent completed a checkout, and so on. It’s not limited to raw error detection. It returns findings as JSON in a schema users can freely define. We call these signal events. Once you’re analyzing every trace, you also have too many individual events to read. That’s why we automatically group similar signal events into clusters. You can see recurring problems and how often they happen, then drill into individual events and the traces behind them. You can use traces from a cluster to build an eval dataset for that type of failure. You can then use your favourite coding agent to hillclimb on those evals and check that the fix works across these cases before shipping it to prod. Trace → analyze → catch errors and form clusters → fix → release. Making this loop easy is why we built Laminar. We’ve put the same thought into the rest of Laminar. Our blog (https://laminar.sh/blog) explains how looking closely at how agents actually work led us to build 20x trace compression, a custom SQL query engine and automatic task extraction. Laminar is Apache 2.0 and self-hostable (https://github.com/lmnr-ai/lmnr). We make money from the managed cloud. We’d love to hear how you track your agents’ performance today. How much do you learn from user feedback versus your own monitoring? And when you fix something, how do you check that it hasn’t introduced a different failure? Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50154029 Points: 39 # Comments: 17"}, :excerpts [{:text "... (https://laminar.sh/blog/flow-1), it matches GPT-6-sol in detection quality while being 23x...", :matched-terms ["gpt"], :source :content} {:text "... what the agent was trying to do and inspect its LLM calls and tool results. Simply looking at the...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... to read or extract value from. Using another LLM to inspect traces is THE solution, but capable...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://building.nu.com/?p=38473", :title "The code was the easy part: Lessons from contributing to the Clojure language", :link "https://building.nu.com/the-code-was-the-easy-part-lessons-from-contributing-to-the-clojure-language/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T13:51:12.000-00:00", :content "Written by: Arthur Fücher I always wanted to contribute to Clojure. Not building a new library, but improving the language itself. Working on a programming language felt too hard to me, a senior software engineer. During a mentorship at Nubank, I told Alex Miller that I hoped to reach that point someday. His response was roughly: “You can do that today.” Not everyone has the opportunity to have a Clojure core contributor mentor them. Alex’s point was simple: the tickets are public, the contribution process is clear, and there are real problems to solve now. The mentorship became: pick an open ticket, work on it, learn. And I worked on two Clojure core tickets. CLJ-2897, about dynamic library loading through prepl, merged and included in Clojure 1.13.0-alpha5. CLJ-1548, about primitive type hints in protocols, still in review when I presented this experience at Clojure/Conj 2026. I expected the code to be the hardest part, and it was hard. But it wasn’t the hardest part: that turned out to be four questions. Questions that I had to answer before anyone would even look at my code. 1. What is actually wrong? My first ticket involved Clojure’s prepl, a programmatic REPL. Like a conventional read-eval-print loop, it accepts expressions and evaluates them. Unlike a conventional REPL, it emits structured results. A developer can connect to a running process through a socket and evaluate forms.. Using add-lib, which add dependencies to a running REPL session, failed in prepl: That was an odd message to receive while using a REPL. The ticket identified the immediate cause: prepl did not bind *repl* to true. My first patch added the binding. It was a two-line change that addressed the ticket’s stated cause. I attached the patch. I felt great. Then Alex pushed me to consider what the user wanted to do. They didn’t want a particular value for *repl*; they wanted to load a library. I applied the patch and tested, the original error disappeared. But now another error message: Context classloader is not a DynamicClassLoader. Same user. Same goal. Another error. add-lib requires a DynamicClassLoader. Prepl hadn’t set one up. My revised patch added both the *repl* binding and the classloader setup. Now, the prepl session could then add Cheshire, require cheshire.core, and call it. The first patch removed the reported error, while the second made the user’s task work. I had even seen a reference to the related classloader issue, CLJ-2540, in the original ticket. I read it without understanding how central it was. Reading a ticket and absorbing a ticket are different things. 2. Why does the current behavior exist? Once the patch worked, I needed to understand why prepl lacked those pieces in the first place. The explanation was historical. Prepl was created in 2018 by copying and modifying clojure.main/repl. But it didn’t keep the REPL’s DynamicClassLoader setup. Later, in 2023, clojure.main/repl gained the *repl* binding, but the change didn’t reach prepl. Two implementations with related responsibilities had drifted apart. This was a consequence of how the code had evolved.Understanding this raised a bigger question: should fix the drift? Or the symptom of the drift? My second ticket exposed a different kind of mismatch. In Clojure, we have primitive type hints. It tells the compiler to use a primitive-aware invocation rather than boxing a value. With ordinary functions, that can be a useful optimization. But consider this protocol example from CLJ-1548: Defining the protocol works. Extending it for strings works. Directly calling the function fails. The reason is subtle. defprotocol accepts the primitive hint but doesn’t generate a corresponding primitive-specialized method. Yet the hint remains in the function’s :arglists metadata. When the compiler reads that metadata and selects a specialized invocation. In this case IFn$OLO, an interface the generated protocol function doesn’t implement. The JVM cast fails before dispatch to the string implementation can happen. The function’s metadata promises a calling convention that the generated function cannot fulfill. Clojure 1.12 already included a fix for a similar issue, about primitive return hints, CLJ-2698. But CLJ-1548 concerned primitive parameter hints, and understanding the difference mattered. “Why does this happen” is not a curiosity question. It’s the only way to decide whether the fix belongs.. 3. What has already been considered? Both tickets contained more accumulated reasoning than I first appreciated. The prepl ticket had pointed to CLJ-2540 all along. A triage process expanded the protocol ticket with the related return-hint precedent. and possible solutions. I wasn’t arriving at untouched problems. Other contributors had already done important investigative work. And yet simply reading those notes wasn’t enough. My first prepl patch proved that I could read a clue and still underestimate it. After the revised prepl patch worked, Alex encouraged me to implement alternatives. Not only think about them. And we ended up comparing three approaches: Copy the missing behavior into prepl. Add *repl* and the DynamicClassLoader setup. The patch was small, five added lines and four removed, but it wouldn’t prevent future drift. Rebuild prepl around clojure.main/repl. Reuse the existing REPL’s extension points, potentially reducing divergence. Experimenting with this revealed complications involving shared state between read and evaluation phases, prompt behavior.. The prototype involved 65 additions and 44 removals. Extract shared classloader setup. Introduce a reusable macro used by both REPL implementations. This provided limited reuse but still didn’t eliminate the drift. The experiments mattered because they replaced an attractive architectural idea with concrete trade-offs. An approach can look elegant until it has to preserve existing exception handling, output structure, and other behavior that real clients expect. In total, I wrote 126 lines of experimental code that wouldn’t be part of the accepted change. And I want to be clear: it wasn’t a waste. It was the cost of knowing different dimensions of the solution. You can’t know the simple option is correct until you’ve seen what the complicated ones actually cost. 4. What quietly rules most options out? The approach that best prevented future prepl/REPL drift wasn’t selected. Rebuilding prepl on top of clojure.main/repl offered the clearest reuse story. But getting the behavior right will need modifying repl itself. And repl has external users who may depend on details of its existing behavior. Changing it to fix prepl would impose risk on code that hadn’t experienced the original bug. We chose the smaller, less disruptive patch. It solved dynamic library loading in prepl without claiming to solve future divergence. We documented that limitation rather than hidden it. The protocol ticket made compatibility even more concrete. One proposed solution was to reject unsupported primitive hints at definition time. To me it was obvious: Why let developers define something that will fail when called? But the answer was: Because not every call fails. Let’s check this example using a protocol function named add (the definition is omitted here): In a direct call, the compiler sees the primitive-hint metadata and calls .invokePrim.. Passing the function as a value uses plain IFn.invoke, not invokePrim. That means there may be code with primitive hints on protocol methods that has worked fine for years.Rejecting those definitions would break code that currently runs. So, the alternatives had different consequences for existing users. On both tickets I had more than one option that worked. What was decided between them had less to do with the code, and a lot to do with everything around it. The final challenge: Explaining the reasoning By this point, I had spent time reconstructing behavior, implementing alternatives. Ready for the next step: Screening/prescreening process. I thought I had done the difficult work. Then Michael Fogus screened the protocol ticket and left six comments. None were about the code. They concerned the description: put the problem first, introduce context in the order a new reader needs it, and make the argument for the change easier to follow. I rewrote the ticket’s explanation, but the patch stayed the same. That feedback connected with something Peter Naur wrote in Programming as Theory Building (1985): “[…]programming properly should be regarded as an activity by which the programmers form or achieve a certain kind of insight, a theory, of the matters at hand.” And he says one more thing: the theory lives in people. It does not fit entirely in the text. So if the theory is in my head, and the decision is in someone else’s, how do I move it across? The four questions I had been answering were ways of building a story. A story that moves a theory from one head to another. The evolution of CLJ-2897 made the contrast visible. Its initial description had 82 words; the final one had 505. Alongside, were 9 lines of accepted code and 126 lines of experimental work written to be thrown away. It shows that for a patch to move, the story, the theory had to be in the head of the person deciding. A ticket isn’t paperwork, it is the transfer of understanding. What contributing to Clojure core taught me The prepl patch was ready in August 2025 and reached Clojure 1.13.0-alpha5 in July 2026. The protocol ticket, opened in October 2014, was still in progress when I wrote this.. That pace was frustrating. And it’s slow for the exact reason I am sharing about this experience. Nobody there accepts code before they accept the theory. I came into this experience prepared to write code. What I wasn’t ready for were the four questions. And the fifth thing: writing it down as a story that builds the theory in someone else’s head. Those activities were the real contribution, alongside the code. I still think about Alex telling me that I could work on Clojure today. He wasn’t promising a quick patch or an easy review. He was pointing out that I could start learning from a real problem immediately. Want to contribute? Explore Clojure’s issue discussions, its ticket tracker, and the official contribution workflow. Choose a problem and investigate what people are actually trying to accomplish. Read the history. Try the alternatives. Explain what you learn. You don’t need a mentor to ask what the user is actually trying to do. You can do that today. The post The code was the easy part: Lessons from contributing to the Clojure language appeared first on Building Nubank."}, :excerpts [{:text "... the easy part: Lessons from contributing to the Clojure language", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :title} {:text "... Arthur Fücher I always wanted to contribute to Clojure. Not building a new library, but improving the...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... Not everyone has the opportunity to have a Clojure core contributor mentor them. Alex’s point was...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://kirahowe.com/2026/oct/19/introducing-volley", :title "volley 0.1.0-alpha1", :link "https://kirahowe.com/2026/oct/19/introducing-volley", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T17:07:11.000-00:00", :content "I made a test runner for Clojure! Clojure is still my language of choice and I&aposve been writing most of my personal projects with it (or babashka) lately, and especially in the babashka projects have been wishing for a better test running experience. I use vitest at work, so it inspired a lot of the functionality of volley. It&aposs a runner for clojure.test tests and currently works on babashka and JVM Clojure. It runs test namespaces in parallel worker processes, shows each namespace as it finishes, and has a watch mode that reruns only the tests that change. It&aposs compatible with kaocha, since that&aposs what I was mostly using before, so switching is just changing one command. With AI generating more and more of my code these days, I find I&aposm running more and more tests, and was wanting a runner that could parallelize large test suites where that results in noticeable speedup. This is what volley looks like running its own test suite: Try it out volley is on Clojars as com.kirahowe/volley. For bb projects, add it to bb.edn with a task: {:paths [\"src\" \"test\"]\n :deps {com.kirahowe/volley {:mvn/version \"0.1.0-alpha.1\"}}\n :tasks {test {:doc \"Run the tests\"\n               :requires ([volley.main])\n               :task (apply volley.main/-main *command-line-args*)}}}\n On JVM Clojure projects that already use kaocha, swap lambdaisland/kaocha for com.kirahowe/volley in :extra-deps, and change kaocha.runner to volley.main in :main-opts, and leave the rest alone. A bin/kaocha binstub that runs clojure -M:test \"$@\" should keep working. How it works The parent process plans the run and hands namespaces to workers, which stay alive across runs. By default, it runs one worker per CPU, capped at 8 for bb and 4 on the JVM. --workers N overrides these, and --isolate gives every namespace a fresh worker. Workers run on whichever runtime the parent uses, or you can specify directly with --runtime bb|jvm. Timings are saved in .volley/, so later runs start the slowest namespaces first. In a terminal, volley re-draws one line per running namespace. When stdout is piped, it prints plain lines instead. Failures show expected and actual values, a structural diff for = assertions, captured output, and the call stack without the bb interpreter&aposs frames. A failed defspec property test shows its seed and shrunk input. The run ends with the slowest tests and prints a command that reruns just the failures. --watch polls test and source paths, works out from ns forms which test namespaces depend on the file you changed, and reruns only those. Keys let you rerun everything, rerun failures, or filter by namespace or test name. Most of kaocha&aposs options and config are supported by default. Suite ids, --focus and --skip (and their -meta forms), --fail-fast, the reader tags in tests.edn, :kaocha/bindings, pending tests, the hooks plugin, JUnit XML, custom reporters, the --print-* flags, and kaocha&aposs exit codes should all work as expected. Performance So far I&aposve only tested this on my busy 16-core M4 max MacBook Pro, but I have found huge improvements on big test suites. In one bb project with 507 tests runs went from ~150s to ~30s with 8 workers, on another JVM Clojure project I saw improvements from ~215s to 40s. So far small JVM Clojure test suites don&apost see much improvement in my experience. This is on my radar to improve, but for now if your tests run in under about 10s, you might not see any improvement with volley because the JVM startup overhead dominates the timing. Known limitations Only handles clojure.test for now. There&aposs no ClojureScript and no coverage. Kaocha plugins such as the notifier, gc-profiling and cloverage are accepted but do nothing. volley prints one warning pointing this out. --randomize and --seed are accepted with a warning. This release doesn&apost randomize test order. volley departs from Kaocha on purpose in a few places: load errors, System/exit inside a test, fail-fast inside a test, load order, and the --print-* maps. There are more details in the \"Deliberate divergences from Kaocha\" section here. This is the very first alpha release so there are still many rough edges, but I&aposm using it all day every day on my own projects so figured it&aposs ready \"enough\" to put out there. My goal is to keep improving it to fix all the paper cuts. If you try it and run into any, I would love to know about it. Please feel free to create issues in the repo or message me in any of the Clojure community places I lurk. Thanks for reading! release / github.com / #babashka #clojure #developer-tools #open-source #oss #performance #release #testing #volley"}, :excerpts [{:text "I made a test runner for Clojure! Clojure is still my language of choice and I&aposve been...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... use vitest at work, so it inspired a lot of the functionality of volley. It&aposs a runner for clojure.test tests and currently works on babashka and JVM Clojure. It runs test namespaces in parallel...", :matched-terms ["functional" "clojure" "jvm"], :source :content} {:text "... using before, so switching is just changing one command. With AI generating more and more of my code...", :matched-terms ["command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T22:02:43.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T22:02:43.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]